'''auspical†''' (âs′pi-kal), ''a.'' [< L. ''auspicalis'', < ''auspex'', a diviner: see ''auspex''.] Auspicatory; pertaining to omens or auspices. ''Blount.''

'''auspicate''' (âs′pi-kāt), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''auspicated'', ppr. ''auspicating''. [< L. ''auspicatus'', pp. of ''auspicari'', make a beginning for the sake of a good omen, begin, prop. take the auspices, act as auspex, < ''auspex'', a diviner: see ''auspex''. Cf. ''augurate''.] '''1.''' To be an augury of; foreshow.
{{smaller block|<poem>Long mayst thou live, and see me thus appear,
As ominous a comet, from my sphere,
Unto thy reign, as that did ''auspicate''
So lasting glory to Augustus’ state.</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', King James’s Coronation Entertainment.}}}}
{{smaller block|There are yet other special auguries of this great change, ''auspicating'', in the natural Progress of Man, the abandonment of all international Preparations for War.
{{right|''Sumner'', Orations, I. 111.}}}}
'''2.''' To initiate or inaugurate with ceremonies calculated to insure good luck. {{smaller|This meaning of the word was borrowed from the Roman practice of taking the auspices before undertaking any important business.}}
{{smaller block|If we are conscious of our situation, and glow with zeal to fill our place as becomes our station and ourselves, we ought to ''auspicate'' all our public proceedings on America with the old warning of the Church, ''Sursum corda!''
{{right|''Burke'', Conciliation with America.}}}}
{{smaller block|To ''auspicate'' . . . the . . . concern and set it agoing with a lustre. {{right|''Lamb'', Ellistoniana.}}}}
'''3.''' To begin or introduce in a favorable or auspicious manner. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The London company merits the praise of having ''auspicated'' liberty in America. ''Bancroft'', Hist. U. S., I. 125.}}
